%% chapter-59.ltx — Effect-Aware IR
%%

\label{ch:effect-ir}

An effect-aware IR tracks the side effects of operations---I/O, memory allocation, synchronization, exceptions---as first-class information in the intermediate representation. By making effects explicit, the compiler can reason about purity, reorder operations safely, and specialize code based on effect information. This chapter develops the design and implementation of effect-aware IRs, from simple effect annotations to full effect-specialized compilation.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 59.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Tracking Effects in IR}\label{sec:tracking-effects}

In a traditional IR, effects are implicit: a store instruction modifies memory, a call may perform arbitrary side effects. An effect-aware IR makes these effects explicit.

\subsection{Effect Annotations on Operations}\label{sec:effect-annotations}

Each operation in the IR carries an effect annotation describing what effects it may perform:

\begin{verbatim}
  %x = add i32 %a, %b          // pure: no effects
  %y = load i32* %p             // reads: memory read
  store i32 %v, i32* %p         // writes: memory write
  %z = call @foo(%x)            // effects: {read, write, io} (conservative)
  %w = call @pure_fn(%x)        // effects: {} (pure, verified by analysis)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect Lattices in IR}\label{sec:effect-lattices-ir}

The IR defines an effect lattice that classifies operations:

