\end{figure}

\subsection{Effect Propagation}\label{sec:effect-propagation}

Effects propagate through the IR: a composite operation has the union of the effects of its components.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Block B1:
  %x = add i32 %a, %b          // effects: {}
  %y = load i32* %p             // effects: {read}
  %z = call @foo(%x)            // effects: {read, write, io}
  // Block effects: {read, write, io} (union of all instructions)
\end{verbatim}

%% ============================================================
%% Section 59.2
%% ============================================================
\section{Effect Annotations on Operations}\label{sec:effect-ops}

Effect annotations can be attached to individual operations, basic blocks, or entire functions.

\subsection{Operation-Level Effects}\label{sec:op-level}

Each operation carries a set of effect tags:

\begin{verbatim}
  %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]
  %y = load i32* %p [read]
  store i32 %v, i32* %p [write]
  %z = call @foo(%x) [read, write, io]
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Function-Level Effects}\label{sec:fn-level}

Functions declare their effects in their signature:

\begin{verbatim}
  define i32 @pure_add(i32 %a, i32 %b) [pure] {
    %sum = add i32 %a, %b
    ret i32 %sum
  }

  define void @write_file(i8* %path, i8* %data) [io, write] {
    // ...
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Block-Level Effects}\label{sec:block-level}

Basic blocks carry the union of their operations' effects, plus any effects from terminator instructions (branches, returns).

