\section{Effect-Based Optimization}\label{sec:effect-optimization}

Effect information enables optimizations that are impossible or difficult without it.

\subsection{Dead Code Elimination}\label{sec:effect-dce}

Pure operations with no uses can be eliminated. Operations with effects cannot be eliminated even if their results are unused (the effects must be preserved).

\begin{verbatim}
  %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]     // no uses -> can be eliminated
  %y = load i32* %p [read]       // no uses -> can be eliminated (if no volatile)
  call @foo() [io]               // no uses -> CANNOT be eliminated (has IO effect)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Code Motion}\label{sec:effect-code-motion}

Pure operations can be moved freely (hoisted out of loops, sunk to their uses). Effectful operations can only be moved within the constraints of their effects.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before:
  for i = 0 to N:
    %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]     // can be hoisted
    %y = load i32* %p [read]       // can be hoisted if %p doesn't change
    store i32 %v, i32* %q [write]  // cannot be hoisted (changes each iteration)

  // After:
  %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]       // hoisted
  %y = load i32* %p [read]         // hoisted (if safe)
  for i = 0 to N:
    store i32 %v, i32* %q [write]  // stays in loop
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Common Subexpression Elimination}\label{sec:effect-cse}

Two pure operations with the same inputs produce the same result and can be merged. Two effectful operations cannot be merged even if their inputs are the same (the effects must be performed each time).

\subsection{Inlining Decisions}\label{sec:effect-inlining}

Effect information guides inlining decisions:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Pure functions are always good candidates for inlining (no side effects to worry about).
  \item Functions with memory effects may be inlined if the memory effects are compatible with the call site.
  \item Functions with IO effects are rarely inlined (IO is expensive and ordering-sensitive).
\end{itemize}

