\section{Effect Specialization}\label{sec:effect-specialization}

Effect specialization generates different code for different effect profiles. A function that is called with both pure and effectful arguments can be specialized for each case.

\subsection{Pure vs. Effectful Specialization}\label{sec:pure-effectful}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Generic:
  fn map(f: A -> B, xs: [A]) -> [B] {
    xs.iter().map(f).collect()
  }

  // Specialized for pure f:
  fn map_pure(f: A -> B [pure], xs: [A]) -> [B] {
    // Can parallelize, vectorize, or fuse with consumers
    xs.par_iter().map(f).collect()
  }

  // Specialized for effectful f:
  fn map_io(f: A -> B [io], xs: [A]) -> [B] {
    // Must execute sequentially, preserving IO order
    xs.iter().map(f).collect()
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect-Directed Dispatch}\label{sec:effect-dispatch}

At call sites, the compiler can dispatch to the appropriate specialization based on the callee's effect profile:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Call site:
  map(|x| x + 1, xs)  // pure closure -> use map_pure
  map(|x| println(x), xs)  // IO closure -> use map_io
\end{verbatim}

