\section{Examples: Pure Computation, IO, Allocation, Synchronization}\label{sec:effect-examples}

\subsection{Pure Computation}\label{sec:pure-computation}

Pure computations have no effects and can be freely optimized:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Pure: can be CSE'd, hoisted, parallelized
  %result = call @compute(%input) [pure]
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{IO}\label{sec:io-effect}

IO effects constrain ordering and prevent certain optimizations:

\begin{verbatim}
  // IO: cannot be reordered with other IO, cannot be eliminated
  call @printf(i8* %format, ...) [io]
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Allocation}\label{sec:alloc-effect}

Allocation effects can be optimized by escape analysis:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Allocation: can be stack-allocated if the object doesn't escape
  %obj = call @malloc(i64 32) [alloc]
  // If %obj doesn't escape -> stack allocate
  // If %obj escapes -> heap allocate
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Synchronization}\label{sec:sync-effect}

Synchronization effects (locks, atomics) constrain reordering:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Synchronization: cannot be reordered with memory operations
  call @lock(%mutex) [sync_write]
  // critical section
  call @unlock(%mutex) [sync_write]
\end{verbatim}

