\section{Advanced Effect-Based Optimization}\label{sec:effect-optimization-advanced}

\subsection{Effect-Guided Loop Optimization}\label{sec:effect-loop-opts}

Effect information is particularly valuable for loop optimization, where the distinction between pure and effectful operations determines what transformations are legal.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before optimization:
  for i = 0 to N {
    %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]           // loop-invariant, pure
    %y = load i32* %p [read]             // loop-invariant if %p not modified
    %z = call @compute(%x) [pure]        // loop-invariant, pure
    store i32 %z, i32* %q [write]        // must stay in loop
  }

  // After effect-guided hoisting:
  %x = add i32 %a, %b [pure]             // hoisted (pure, loop-invariant)
  %y = load i32* %p [read]               // hoisted (read, %p not modified in loop)
  %z = call @compute(%x) [pure]          // hoisted (pure, loop-invariant)
  for i = 0 to N {
    store i32 %z, i32* %q [write]        // stays (write effect)
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect-Based Parallelization}\label{sec:effect-parallelization}

Effect information determines whether a loop can be parallelized:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Effect-Guided Parallelization}\label{alg:effect-parallel}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{CanParallelize}{$\mathrm{loop}$}
  \State $\mathrm{effects} \gets \mathrm{collect\_effects}(\mathrm{loop})$
  \If{$\mathrm{effects} \cap \{\mathrm{io}, \mathrm{sync}\} \neq \emptyset$}
    \State \Return \textbf{false} \Comment{IO or sync prevents parallelization}
  \EndIf
  \If{$\mathrm{effects} \cap \{\mathrm{write}\} \neq \emptyset$}
    \State \Comment{Check for write-write or write-read conflicts}
    \State $\mathrm{conflicts} \gets \mathrm{analyze\_dependencies}(\mathrm{loop})$
    \If{$\mathrm{conflicts} \neq \emptyset$}
      \State \Return \textbf{false}
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
  \State \Return \textbf{true} \Comment{Pure or read-only: safe to parallelize}
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Parallelizable (pure body):
  for i = 0 to N {
    result[i] = f(input[i])  // pure: no side effects
  }
  // -> parallel_for i = 0 to N { result[i] = f(input[i]) }

  // Not parallelizable (write conflicts):
  for i = 0 to N {
    total += input[i]  // write to 'total' creates a dependency
  }
  // -> must use reduction: parallel_reduce(+, input)

  // Not parallelizable (IO):
  for i = 0 to N {
    println(input[i])  // IO: order matters
  }
  // -> must execute sequentially
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect Specialization in Practice}\label{sec:effect-specialization-practice}

Effect specialization generates different versions of a function for different effect profiles:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Generic function:
  fn for_each<T>(xs: &[T], f: impl Fn(&T)) {
    for x in xs { f(x); }
  }

  // Specialized for pure f:
  fn for_each_pure<T>(xs: &[T], f: impl Fn(&T) [pure]) {
    // Can vectorize, unroll, or parallelize
    xs.par_iter().for_each(f);
  }

  // Specialized for IO f:
  fn for_each_io<T>(xs: &[T], f: impl Fn(&T) [io]) {
    // Must execute sequentially
    for x in xs { f(x); }
  }

  // Call site dispatch:
  for_each(data, |x| x * 2);        // pure -> for_each_pure
  for_each(data, |x| println(x));   // IO   -> for_each_io
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect-Based Inlining Decisions}\label{sec:effect-inlining-detail}

Effect information guides inlining decisions with a cost model:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Effect-based inlining decisions}\label{tab:effect-inlining}
\begin{tabular}{p{2.5cm}p{2.5cm}p{3cm}p{3.5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Callee Effect} & \textbf{Inline?} & \textbf{Reason} & \textbf{Caveat} \\
\hline
Pure & Always & No side effects to worry about & Code size \\
Read only & Usually & Enables hoisting after inlining & May expose aliasing \\
Write & Sometimes & May enable store forwarding & Ordering constraints \\
IO & Rarely & IO ordering is fragile & Only if small \\
Alloc & Depends & Escape analysis after inline & May enable stack alloc \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Effect Analysis for Exception Safety}\label{sec:effect-exception}

Effect tracking can be extended to reason about exception safety:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Effect analysis for exception safety:
  fn process() [exn] {
    let resource = acquire();     // [alloc]
    let result = compute(resource); // [pure, exn]
    release(resource);             // [write]
    result
  }

  // Problem: if compute() throws, release() is never called
  // Effect analysis detects: resource leak on exception path

  // Fix: use RAII or try-finally
  fn process() [exn] {
    let resource = acquire();     // [alloc]
    let result = try {
      compute(resource)           // [pure, exn]
    } finally {
      release(resource);          // [write] - always executed
    };
    result
  }
\end{verbatim}

